 I'm glad you found my explanation helpful! Here's an example of natural selection using a simple MATLAB simulation. This simulation will model the survival of two different genotypes (represented by "A" and "B") based on their environmental fitness.
```MATLAB
% Set initial population sizes and genotype frequencies
N = 100; % total population size
pA = 0.6; % frequency of genotype A
pB = 0.4; % frequency of genotype B
% Initialize random population with genotypes
population = randi([1, 2], N, 1); % 1 for genotype A, 2 for genotype B
% Simulate natural selection over multiple generations
numGenerations = 50;
for i = 1:numGenerations
    % Calculate fitness based on genotype
    fitnessA = 0.8 * pA; % fitness of genotype A is 0.8 times its frequency
    fitnessB = 0.5 * pB; % fitness of genotype B is 0.5 times its frequency
    % Calculate total population fitness
    totalFitness = fitnessA * sum(population == 1) + fitnessB * sum(population == 2);

    % Calculate probabilities of producing offspring based on fitness
    probabilities = [fitnessA / totalFitness, fitnessB / totalFitness];

    % Generate new generation via roulette wheel selection
    newPopulation = ceil(cumsum(rand(size(probabilities)) .* repmat(probabilities, 1, N)) .* N);

    % Update genotype frequencies
    pA = sum(newPopulation == 1) / N;
    pB = 1 - pA;
end
% Print final genotype frequencies
fprintf('Genotype frequencies after %d generations:\n', numGenerations);
fprintf('Genotype A: %.2f\n', pA);
fprintf('Genotype B: %.2f\n', pB);
```
